  "final_response": "- Git uses different authentication mechanisms depending on the remote‑URL protocol you use.  \n- Smart HTTP runs over standard HTTPS ports and can employ a variety of HTTP authentication methods, most commonly username/password authentication[protocols.006-smart-http].  \n- When you push to a repository that requires authentication, Git will typically prompt you for a username and password; these credentials can be cached with tools such as Keychain on macOS or Credential Manager on Windows[protocols.009-the-cons].  \n- The same HTTPS URL can be used for anonymous read‑only operations (cloning public projects) and for authenticated write operations (pushing) once you supply valid credentials[1-setting-up-account.002-ssh-access].",
